import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Callout
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths
import ../../../Ui/PageHero

meta = {
	title: "Kv: values in memory",
	description: "Kv on the Roc Solid server platform: typed values by key in the server's memory, with a time to live and atomic operations, for rate limits, caches and one-time tokens.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "counting", label: "Counting: a rate limit" },
		{ id: "once", label: "Once: a sign-in's state" },
		{ id: "operations", label: "The operations" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="The platform · Kv"
				title="Values in memory, by key"
			>
				<code>Kv</code> keeps values by key in the server's memory, for as long as you say or until the
				server stops. Nothing goes to disk: a restart forgets everything. Use it for what is cheap to lose
				and read on many requests: rate-limit counts, a cache, a one-time token. A value is any type JSON
				can encode, read back as the type its reader asks for.
			</PageHero>

			<Heading id="counting">Counting: a rate limit</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_kv_limit.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_kv_limit.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>Kv.increment!</code> counts atomically: two requests at once both count. A counter's time to
				live starts when it is made and later increments don't move it, so it counts in windows: here, one
				minute from an address's first try.
			</p>

			<Heading id="once">Once: a sign-in's state</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_kv_once.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_kv_once.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>Kv.take!</code> returns a value and forgets it in one step. The state another site sends back
				completes one sign-in; a second use finds nothing. Of two requests taking one key at once, one gets
				the value, the other <code>NotFound</code>.
			</p>

			<Heading id="operations">The operations</Heading>
			<p>
				Every operation is atomic. Each that keeps a value takes a time to live,
				<code>{"{ ttl_ms }"}</code>; 0, the default, keeps it until the server stops.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<code>{"Kv.set!(key, value, { ttl_ms })"}</code> keeps a value, replacing what was there.
					<code>Kv.get!(key)</code> reads it: <code>NotFound</code> if missing or expired,
					<code>BadValue(why)</code> if it isn't the type asked for. <code>Kv.delete!(key)</code> forgets it.
				</li>
				<li>
					<code>Kv.increment!(key, by, opts)</code> adds to an integer (from 0) and returns the new value:
					counts and rate limits.
				</li>
				<li>
					<code>Kv.add!(key, value, opts)</code> keeps a value only if the key is empty, else answers
					<code>Exists</code>. Of two requests adding one key, one wins: a claim, or a request seen once (an
					idempotency key).
				</li>
				<li><code>Kv.take!(key)</code> reads a value and forgets it: a one-time token.</li>
				<li>
					<code>Kv.swap!(key, was, value, opts)</code> replaces a value only if it is still
					<code>was</code>, else answers <code>Changed</code>. To update a value from its old one (a tally, a
					list of overrides), read, swap, and on <code>Changed</code> read again and retry, so concurrent
					updates never lose one.
				</li>
				<li>
					<code>{"Kv.push!(key, value, { keep })"}</code> puts a value at the front of a capped list,
					keeping its newest <code>keep</code>; <code>Kv.list!(key)</code> reads it, newest first.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				The store holds 64 MiB (<code>--kv-max-mb</code>, or <code>KV_MAX_MB</code>); past that, the least
				recently used values go, so a cache can't grow without end. By convention, a key starts with its
				owner (<code>login:…</code>, <code>email:mailbox</code>), so a package's keys never meet an app's.
				An app runs as one server, so there is one store, with no network between it and your code.
			</p>
			<p>
				What must survive a restart belongs in a table (<a href={Paths.platform_database}>the database</a>)
				or an <a href={Paths.platform_actors}>actor</a>. Every kind of state, side by side:
				<a href={"${Paths.platform_live}#which"}>which to use</a>.
			</p>
			<Callout kind="tip" title="Tested">
				<p>
					The platform's tests let a value expire, count 200 increments at once, cap a list, and pass the
					limit to see the least recently used values go.
				</p>
			</Callout>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
